        "text_gcWm8i": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>We make denim clothing in Portland, Oregon. Not much of it, and not quickly.</p><p>It's cut to look right when you aren't thinking about it — tired, off the clock, shirt half unbuttoned. If a garment only holds up while you're standing straight and paying attention, it doesn't hold up.</p><p>Most of the work is fit. A high rise that sits where a waist actually is. Snug through the seat and thigh. A flare at the ankle because a boot has to go somewhere. None of it is meant to be visible. If you notice the pattern-making, we did it badly.</p><p>Thirteen-ounce indigo from Nihon Menpu in Japan, cut and sewn under a pattern-maker with thirty years in the trade. One garment at a time, after you order it. Three to four weeks. Nothing sits in a warehouse.</p><p>When we get something wrong we change it, and the number moves. CJ.101.<strong>1</strong> becomes CJ.101.<strong>2</strong>. The pair you own stays the pair you own. Most of the changes are small enough that only we would notice.</p><p>If you want something altered, ask when you order. Sometimes the answer is yes.</p><p>The studio is in Portland. If you're nearby, come by.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
